      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t think so, but there could be some difference. It\u0027s nicer to do the return migration in the wakeup path as the rq lock juggling is a little cleaner (ie: we\u0027re not taking additional locks). But doing the return migration from __schedule() means we have to drop the current_rq lock, acquire and drop the target_rq lock,and then re-acquire the current_rq lock, before starting the selection process again.\n\nBut technically the number of lock acquisitions should be the same (if ttwu was trying while __schedule was on another CPU, ttwu would have to wait for __schedule to drop the lock before it can proceed and __schedule would have to try again anyway).\n\nAdditionally, it only triggers to resolve the edge case I haven\u0027t sorted, so I\u0027m not expecting it to be frequent.",
